              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "In English, **hosanna** is defined as an exclamation of praise, adoration, or joy, typically directed toward God. \n\nHowever, the word has a rich history, and its meaning has evolved over thousands of years from a desperate plea into a shout of celebration.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Original Hebrew Meaning: \"Save, Please!\"\nLinguistically, \"hosanna\" is the English transliteration of the Hebrew phrase *Hoshia-na* (\u05d4\u05d5\u05b9\u05e9\u05b4\u05c1\u05d9\u05e2\u05b8\u05d4\u05be\u05e0\u05b8\u05bc\u05d0) or Aramaic *Hosha'na*. \n* **Hoshia** comes from the Hebrew root meaning \"to save\" or \"to deliver.\"\n* **Na** is a particle used to express an urgent request, meaning \"please\" or \"now.\"\n\nTherefore, the literal, original definition of hosanna is **\"Save, please!\"** or **\"Save us, now!\"** It was not originally a shout of praise, but rather an urgent plea for rescue or salvation.\n\n### 2. Hosanna in Judaism\nIn the Hebrew Bible (Old Testament), the phrase appears in Psalm 118:25: *\"Lord, save us [Hoshi'ah na]; Lord, grant us success.\"* \n\nIn Jewish tradition, specifically during the festival of **Sukkot** (the Feast of Tabernacles), worshippers carry leafy branches (including palm fronds) and walk in procession around the synagogue altar while chanting *Hoshana* prayers. These prayers plead with God for salvation, deliverance, and agricultural blessing (such as rain).\n\n### 3. Hosanna in Christianity\nThe word took on a dual meaning during Jesus's \"Triumphal Entry\" into Jerusalem (celebrated by Christians as Palm Sunday). \n\nAccording to the Gospels, as Jesus rode into the city on a donkey, crowds gathered, waved palm branches, and shouted:\n> *\"Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest heaven!\"* (Matthew 21:9)\n\nBy calling out \"Hosanna\" to Jesus, the crowd was recognizing Him as the Messiah (the Savior) and essentially crying out, **\"Save us, we pray!\"** (specifically hoping for deliverance from Roman oppression). Because they believed their savior had finally arrived, the plea quickly doubled as a shout of victory and adoration.\n\n### Summary of Modern Usage\nToday, while its historical and theological root is a prayer for salvation (\"Save us!\"), most people use \"hosanna\" in religious liturgies and music as a synonym for **\"Hallelujah\"**\u2014a joyous cry of thanksgiving and praise to God.",
